<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-mutually-exclusive-and-independent-events" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Mutually Exclusive and Independent Events</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>3.0 Statistics and Probability</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>3.2 Probability II</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>Determine probability of mutually exclusive and independent events</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>11</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>40 minutes</p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>12 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    Learners discover the difference between events that can and cannot happen simultaneously, motivating the distinction between mutually exclusive and non-mutually exclusive events.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    After part (b), do not rush to define <q>mutually exclusive</q>. Ask learners: <q>Is there any learner who is in both events R and F? Can you point to one in your diagram?</q> Let their Venn diagram answer the question, then name what they discovered.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-sports-day">
        <title>School Sports Day</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                At a school sports day, each of 30 learners can enter running events (R), field events (F), or both. The teacher records that 18 learners entered running events, 14 entered field events, and 8 entered both.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Draw a Venn diagram with two overlapping circles labelled R and F. Fill in:
                            <ul>
                                <li><p>how many learners are in R only (not F),</p></li>
                                <li><p>how many are in F only (not R),</p></li>
                                <li><p>how many are in both,</p></li>
                                <li><p>how many are in neither.</p></li>
                            </ul>
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <!-- TODO: Add a venn diagram -->
                            <p>
                                R only: <m>18 - 8 = 10</m>. F only: <m>14 - 8 = 6</m>. Both: 8. Neither: <m>30 - (10+8+6) = 6</m>.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            One learner is selected at random. Find <m>P(R)</m>, <m>P(F)</m>, <m>P(R \text{ and } F)</m>, and <m>P(R \text{ or } F)</m>.
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <m>P(R) = \tfrac{18}{30} = \tfrac{3}{5}</m>, <m>P(F) = \tfrac{14}{30} = \tfrac{7}{15}</m>,
                                <m>P(R \cap F) = \tfrac{8}{30} = \tfrac{4}{15}</m>, <m>P(R \cup F) = \tfrac{24}{30} = \tfrac{4}{5}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Can a learner be in both R and F at the same time? What does this tell you about these two events?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                Yes — 8 learners are in both. So R and F can occur simultaneously; they are <em>not</em> mutually exclusive.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Now define two new events: G = <q>selected learner is in running events only</q> and H = <q>selected learner is in field events only</q>. Can G and H both be true for the same learner? What does this mean for the pair (G, H)?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                A learner in <q>running only</q> is definitely not in <q>field only</q> — the events cannot both happen. So G and H are mutually exclusive: <m>P(G \cap H) = 0</m>.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>12 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalise the definitions of mutually exclusive and independent events, and state the corresponding probability rules.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Return to the Venn diagram. Name the key idea: if two events share no outcomes their intersection is empty and <m>P(A \cap B) = 0</m> — these are mutually exclusive. Then introduce independence separately: two events are independent if knowing one happened gives no information about the other, tested by <m>P(A \cap B) = P(A) \times P(B)</m>. Stress that mutually exclusive events with positive probability are <em>never</em> independent, since knowing A occurred guarantees B did not.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <insight>
        <title>Key Ideas</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Two events A and B are <term>mutually exclusive</term> (or <term>disjoint</term>) if they cannot both occur at the same time:
                        <md>P(A \cap B) = 0.</md>
                        For mutually exclusive events, the addition rule simplifies to
                        <md>P(A \cup B) = P(A) + P(B).</md>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Two events A and B are <term>independent</term> if the occurrence of one does not affect the probability of the other. The test for independence is:
                        <md>P(A \cap B) = P(A) \times P(B).</md>
                        If this equation does not hold, the events are <term>dependent</term>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Events that are mutually exclusive (with positive probability) are never independent: if A occurs, B is ruled out, so knowledge of A certainly affects B.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The <term>complement rule</term> (from Grade 10): <m>P(A') = 1 - P(A)</m>, where <m>A'</m> is the event that A does <em>not</em> occur.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <title>Identifying Mutually Exclusive Events</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A single card is drawn at random from a set of 10 cards numbered 1 to 10. Define the events:
                <ul>
                    <li><p>A = <q>card shows an even number</q>,</p></li>
                    <li><p>B = <q>card shows an odd number</q>.</p></li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Are A and B mutually exclusive? Justify your answer.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(A \cup B)</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>What do you notice about <m>P(A) + P(B)</m>?</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            A number cannot be both even and odd, so <m>A \cap B = \{\}</m>, which gives <m>P(A \cap B) = 0</m>. Yes, A and B are mutually exclusive.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Since the events are mutually exclusive:
                            <md>P(A \cup B) = P(A) + P(B) = \frac{5}{10} + \frac{5}{10} = 1.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>P(A) + P(B) = 1</m>. This makes sense: every card is either even or odd, so <q>A or B</q> is a certain event. A and B are <term>complementary</term> events — they are mutually exclusive and together cover all outcomes.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Non-Mutually Exclusive Events</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A card is drawn at random from a set of 10 cards numbered 1 to 10. Define:
                <ul>
                    <li><p>A = <q>card is even</q>,</p></li>
                    <li><p>B = <q>card shows a number greater than 6</q>.</p></li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Show that A and B are <em>not</em> mutually exclusive.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(A \cup B)</m> by listing outcomes.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Verify your answer using the formula <m>P(A \cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \cap B)</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>A \cap B = \{8, 10\}</m> (even and greater than 6), so <m>P(A \cap B) = \frac{2}{10} \neq 0</m>. The events can occur simultaneously, so they are not mutually exclusive.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>A \cup B = \{2, 4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10\}</m> — 7 outcomes, so <m>P(A \cup B) = \frac{7}{10}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>P(A) = \frac{5}{10}</m>, <m>P(B) = \frac{4}{10}</m>, <m>P(A \cap B) = \frac{2}{10}</m>.
                            <md>P(A \cup B) = \frac{5}{10} + \frac{4}{10} - \frac{2}{10} = \frac{7}{10}. \quad \checkmark</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Testing for Independence</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A fair coin is tossed and a fair die is rolled simultaneously. Let:
                <ul>
                    <li><p>A = <q>coin shows heads</q>,</p></li>
                    <li><p>B = <q>die shows a 6</q>.</p></li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>How many equally-likely outcomes are there in total?</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(A)</m>, <m>P(B)</m>, and <m>P(A \cap B)</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Are A and B independent?</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            There are <m>2 \times 6 = 12</m> equally-likely outcomes: (H,1), (H,2), …, (T,6).
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>P(A) = \frac{6}{12} = \frac{1}{2}</m> (6 outcomes with heads). <m>P(B) = \frac{2}{12} = \frac{1}{6}</m> (2 outcomes with a 6: (H,6) and (T,6)).
                            <m>P(A \cap B) = \frac{1}{12}</m> (only (H,6)).
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Check: <m>P(A) \times P(B) = \frac{1}{2} \times \frac{1}{6} = \frac{1}{12} = P(A \cap B)</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Since <m>P(A \cap B) = P(A) \times P(B)</m>, events A and B are <term>independent</term>. This makes intuitive sense — the result of a coin toss has no effect on what a die shows.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Using Independence to Find Probabilities</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Events A and B are independent, with <m>P(A) = 0.3</m> and <m>P(B) = 0.6</m>. Find:
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p><m>P(A \cap B)</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>P(A \cup B)</m></p></li>
                    <li><p>The probability that neither A nor B occurs.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Since A and B are independent:
                            <md>P(A \cap B) = P(A) \times P(B) = 0.3 \times 0.6 = 0.18.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(A \cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \cap B) = 0.3 + 0.6 - 0.18 = 0.72.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(\text{neither}) = 1 - P(A \cup B) = 1 - 0.72 = 0.28.</md>
                            We can verify: since A and B are independent, so are their complements, so <m>P(A') \times P(B') = 0.7 \times 0.4 = 0.28</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A fair die is rolled. Define:
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>A = <q>result is even</q>,</p></li>
                        <li><p>B = <q>result is a multiple of 3</q>.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>List the outcomes in A, in B, and in <m>A \cap B</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Are A and B mutually exclusive?</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find <m>P(A \cup B)</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>A = \{2,4,6\}</m>, <m>B = \{3,6\}</m>, <m>A \cap B = \{6\}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                No — <m>P(A \cap B) = \frac{1}{6} \neq 0</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P(A \cup B) = \frac{3}{6} + \frac{2}{6} - \frac{1}{6} = \frac{4}{6} = \frac{2}{3}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Aisha draws a single token at random from a bag containing 3 red, 4 blue, and 3 green tokens.
                    Let A = <q>red token</q> and B = <q>blue token</q>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Are A and B mutually exclusive? Explain.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find <m>P(A \cup B)</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find <m>P((A \cup B)')</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Yes — a token cannot be both red and blue, so <m>P(A \cap B) = 0</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P(A \cup B) = \frac{3}{10} + \frac{4}{10} = \frac{7}{10}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P((A \cup B)') = 1 - \frac{7}{10} = \frac{3}{10}</m> (probability of green).
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For two events A and B, <m>P(A) = 0.5</m>, <m>P(B) = 0.4</m>, and <m>P(A \cap B) = 0.20</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Are A and B mutually exclusive?</p></li>
                        <li><p>Are A and B independent?</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                No — <m>P(A \cap B) = 0.20 \neq 0</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P(A) \times P(B) = 0.5 \times 0.4 = 0.20 = P(A \cap B)</m>. Yes, A and B are independent.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Events A and B are independent, with <m>P(A) = 0.6</m> and <m>P(B) = 0.4</m>. Find:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>P(A \cap B)</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>P(A \cup B)</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>The probability that neither A nor B occurs.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>P(A \cap B) = 0.6 \times 0.4 = 0.24</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p><m>P(A \cup B) = 0.6 + 0.4 - 0.24 = 0.76</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p><m>P(\text{neither}) = 1 - 0.76 = 0.24</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For two events A and B, <m>P(A) = 0.5</m>, <m>P(B) = 0.3</m>, and <m>P(A \cup B) = 0.65</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Find <m>P(A \cap B)</m>. Are A and B mutually exclusive?</p></li>
                        <li><p>Are A and B independent?</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P(A \cap B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \cup B) = 0.5 + 0.3 - 0.65 = 0.15</m>.
                                Since <m>P(A \cap B) = 0.15 \neq 0</m>, the events are not mutually exclusive.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P(A) \times P(B) = 0.5 \times 0.3 = 0.15 = P(A \cap B)</m>. Yes, A and B are independent.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>

</section>
